Derive calc_ionograms' rank count from the schedule instead of hardcoding it.

**In scheduled mode `-np` is not a tuning knob. It is part of the schedule.**
`calc_ionograms.py:452` reads

    st = conf.sounder_timings[rank]

with no guard, so each MPI rank takes one entry of the outer list. The rank
count and the schedule length are therefore the same number written in two
places -- one in `my_station.ini`, one in this script -- and nothing checks
that they agree.

**Both ways of disagreeing are silent.** Too few ranks and the transmitters
past the cut are never sounded: no error, no warning, and `ionograms.log` looks
exactly as it should because the ranks that do exist are working. Too many and
the surplus rank dies of `IndexError` at startup while the others carry on, so
the process count is wrong but every product that appears is correct. Neither
failure is visible in the thing an operator checks.

This became reachable the moment DOB went to `serendipitous=false`
(2026-08-12). In search mode the timings come from `find_timings.py` and any
`-np` is valid, which is why the coupling never mattered before. It matters
now, and it will matter every time a transmitter is added -- adding Cyprus
already required editing this number by hand, and getting it right was luck
rather than process.

**In search mode the ranks stay free parallelism, and the answer stays 2.**
That is patch 0004's measured value: as a single process `calc_ionograms` lost
4.28% of soundings, and at four ranks the machine saturated badly enough that
the *recorder* lost 63% of its stream. The fallback when the derivation cannot
run is also 2, so a broken `python3` reproduces exactly the behaviour this
patch replaces rather than quietly dropping to one rank.

The launch line now prints the number it derived. `-np` is the one parameter
here whose wrongness is invisible in the log, so it goes in the log.

The agent enforces the same rule from the other side: `services/agent/control.py`
refuses a `set_config` whose schedule length disagrees with the `-np` it reads
out of this script, and stops checking once that `-np` is a variable -- which is
what this patch makes it. See `tests/test_agent.py::test_np_must_match_the_schedule`.

**Do not run any of the changed lines by hand.** `$MPIRUN` and `$CONF_FILE` are
set earlier in the script; pasted at a prompt they expand to nothing, and a
second `calc_ionograms` beside the running one competes for the same ringbuffer.

Anchored at line 141 (`fi`), which assumes 0003 through 0008 are already
applied: `echo "calc_ionograms.py"` at 143, the invocation at 149, and
`echo "station_monitor.py"` at 151. That is DOB's layout as of 2026-08-13.
`git apply` locates by context and will report an offset if it has moved, but
check first:

    git apply --check 0009-dombas-derive-np-from-the-schedule.patch

The derivation itself was exercised against every config shape it can meet:
two ranks -> 2, one rank -> 1, a flat (non-nested) list -> 1, search mode -> 2,
and an unreadable config -> 2.

diff --git a/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh b/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
--- a/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
+++ b/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
@@ -141,12 +141,43 @@ fi
 fi
 
-echo "calc_ionograms.py"
-# Under MPI: this is the process with the ringbuffer deadline, and as a single
-# process it lost 4.28% of soundings. Two ranks, not four -- at four the
-# machine saturated and the RECORDER lost 63% of its stream. It reads
-# its rank from MPI.COMM_WORLD (calc_ionograms.py:99-101), and `sounder_timings`
-# is stored one list per rank for exactly this reason.
-$MPIRUN -np 2 python3 -u calc_ionograms.py --config "$CONF_FILE" > logs/ionograms.log 2>&1 &
+# One transmitter per MPI rank -- `st = conf.sounder_timings[rank]` at
+# calc_ionograms.py:452, with no guard -- so in scheduled mode this count is
+# part of the schedule and not a tuning knob. Hardcoded, every schedule change
+# is two edits, and forgetting the second one fails silently: too few ranks and
+# the transmitters past the cut are never sounded, too many and one rank dies
+# of IndexError while the rest carry on. The log reads as healthy either way,
+# because for the ranks that survive it is.
+#
+# In search mode the timings come from find_timings.py and the ranks are free
+# parallelism, so the answer there is patch 0004's measured 2.
+NP_IONO=$(python3 - "$CONF_FILE" <<'PY'
+import configparser, json, sys
+
+parser = configparser.ConfigParser()
+parser.read(sys.argv[1])
+flag = parser.get("lfm", "serendipitous", fallback="true")
+scheduled = flag.strip().strip('"').lower() in ("false", "0", "no")
+try:
+    timings = json.loads(parser.get("lfm", "sounder_timings", fallback="") or "[]")
+except ValueError:
+    timings = []
+if not scheduled:
+    print(2)
+elif timings and all(isinstance(entry, list) for entry in timings):
+    print(len(timings))
+else:
+    print(1)
+PY
+)
+# An empty result means python3 failed, not that the schedule is empty. 2 is
+# the value this patch replaced, so the fallback is the old behaviour rather
+# than a silent drop to one rank.
+[ -n "$NP_IONO" ] || NP_IONO=2
+
+# The one parameter whose wrongness is invisible in the log, so it goes in the
+# log.
+echo "calc_ionograms.py -np $NP_IONO"
+$MPIRUN -np "$NP_IONO" python3 -u calc_ionograms.py --config "$CONF_FILE" > logs/ionograms.log 2>&1 &
 
 echo "station_monitor.py"
 python3 -u station_monitor.py --config "$CONF_FILE" > logs/station_monitor.log 2>&1 &
